/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  random_placement

<LAND_GENERATION>

  base_terrain GRASS2

  /* PAIR 1 - SAME-ZONE CONTROL. Both lands zone 30, both radius 8. */
  create_land
  {
  terrain_type SNOW
  land_position 15 50
  base_size 2
  number_of_tiles 900
  zone 30
  other_zone_avoidance_distance 8
  }

  create_land
  {
  terrain_type DIRT
  land_position 23 50
  base_size 2
  number_of_tiles 900
  zone 30
  other_zone_avoidance_distance 8
  }

  /* PAIR 2 - DIFFERENT ZONES, SYMMETRIC. Zone 31 radius 6, zone 32 radius 6. */
  create_land
  {
  terrain_type DESERT
  land_position 45 50
  base_size 2
  number_of_tiles 900
  zone 31
  other_zone_avoidance_distance 6
  }

  create_land
  {
  terrain_type DIRT2
  land_position 53 50
  base_size 2
  number_of_tiles 900
  zone 32
  other_zone_avoidance_distance 6
  }

  /* PAIR 3 - DIFFERENT ZONES, ASYMMETRIC. Zone 33 radius 12, zone 34 radius 2. */
  create_land
  {
  terrain_type DIRT3
  land_position 75 50
  base_size 2
  number_of_tiles 900
  zone 33
  other_zone_avoidance_distance 12
  }

  create_land
  {
  terrain_type GRASS3
  land_position 83 50
  base_size 2
  number_of_tiles 900
  zone 34
  other_zone_avoidance_distance 2
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_66 - other_zone_avoidance_distance: same-zone exemption, and which
radius a mismatched pair actually uses
docs/known-issues.md BUG-016, src/preview/generator/lands.ts
(violatesZoneAvoidance, commonFields), docs/preview-design.md Sec.6.1
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
Our model (lands.ts:1221-1258, cited from Sec.6.1 as "within
other_zone_avoidance_distance (the SMALLER of the two lands' values) of a
tile owned by a DIFFERENT zone; zone -12 exempt") has never been checked
against the real engine directly - every existing measurement that touches
this attribute (RMSTEST_25/51) was reading the CROSS-SHAPED ORIGIN REGION and
only incidentally used other_zone_avoidance_distance as min_placement_distance's
default at ORIGIN time. Nothing has measured its effect during GROWTH, which is
where lands.ts actually applies it (a live per-tile check against whatever the
neighbour has claimed so far, not a one-off distance-at-placement check). Filed
as a suspicion under BUG-016 while building Venn.rms, which uses this exact
shape (two same-zone auxiliary lands, non-zero radius, radius differing between
zone pairs) at lines 284/299/312/326.

THREE PAIRS, EACH ON ONE OF THREE COMPETING QUESTIONS

Pair 1 (SNOW zone 30 radius 8, DIRT zone 30 radius 8, origins 16 apart):
SAME zone. Sec.6.1's own sentence says the check is against a DIFFERENT zone
only, so this pair should be able to touch and interleave freely - a land
avoiding its OWN zone would refuse ever to expand. Read the SNOW/DIRT boundary.
  gap ~0 (patches meet/interleave)   same-zone exemption confirmed
  gap ~8 (held apart by the radius)  the exemption is missing; a land is
                                      avoiding tiles by ZONE NUMBER MATCH
                                      rather than skipping same-zone entirely

Pair 2 (DESERT zone 31 radius 6, DIRT2 zone 32 radius 6, origins 16 apart):
Different zones, SYMMETRIC radius. This is the positive control - it has to
show avoidance actually fires before pair 3's asymmetric read means anything.
  gap ~6   avoidance fires as expected; proceed to trust pair 3
  gap ~0   avoidance does not fire during growth at all (only, if anything,
           at ORIGIN placement, which explicit land_position bypasses
           entirely - see lands.ts's early return for the declared-position
           case). If this reads 0, STOP: pair 3 cannot be read meaningfully,
           because a null result there would be this same defect, not an
           answer about which radius wins.

Pair 3 (DIRT3 zone 33 radius 12, GRASS3 zone 34 radius 2, origins 16 apart):
Different zones, ASYMMETRIC radius. The discriminating pair.
  gap ~2    SMALLER of the two wins - confirms the current model as written
  gap ~12   LARGER of the two wins - the model has the comparison backwards
  gap ~0    same failure as pair 2's null reading; avoidance is not enforced
            here either, so this pair's asymmetry never got exercised
  something else, stable but not near 2 or 12   each land is applying only
            ITS OWN radius against the other's tiles (non-pairwise) rather
            than a pairwise min/max - the boundary is then wherever the two
            independent constraints happen to settle, which is not a fixed
            constant and needs a second run design, not a read of this one

READ ARM 2 FIRST. It is the control and gates whether arm 3 is readable at
all - the same discipline BUG-022's RMSTEST_64 used for its own zero-rule
arms.

GENERATE AT NORMAL (200), ANY PLAYER COUNT. THREE RUNS.
Three runs because the boundary is a live contest during growth (round-robin
frontier sampling is randomised - Sec.6.1), and pair 3 in particular could in
principle settle asymmetrically depending on which side reaches the middle
first. A gap that reproduces across three runs is a real number; one that
scatters is itself a finding (the boundary is order-dependent, which argues
against a fixed-radius model entirely).

number_of_tiles 900 per land, base_size 2, on 16-tile-apart origins is
deliberately generous - at that budget an unrestricted circular land reaches
past the midpoint of every pair, so every pair is a genuine contest for the
boundary tile, not two lands that ran out of budget before touching.

READ WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --bbox SNOW --bbox DIRT
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --bbox DESERT --bbox DIRT2
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --bbox DIRT3 --bbox GRASS3
  (--bbox only takes one terrain per invocation; run it once per terrain,
  six calls total.) Each pair's origins share y=100, so the gap is
  max_x(left terrain) to min_x(right terrain) on the two bboxes. Cross-check
  with --patches on each terrain for centroid and area - if a patch's area is
  far below 900, growth was starved rather than blocked, and the gap reading
  for that pair needs a re-run at a larger number_of_tiles rather than being
  taken at face value.

If any pair's two terrains are not both present in the terrain histogram at
all, that land failed to grow (originFallbackCenter or worse) rather than
being avoidance-limited - regenerate before reading.
================================================================================
*/
